# Every layer reachable from a composition through parts, at any depth, with the route that reached
# it. One row per (top layer, reached layer, path), so a layer reachable two ways appears twice, on
# purpose, because the repeat is the finding.
#
# The second level escapes every validator
#   Compositions nest, and the second level is where the model stops being checkable by any
#   validator. A holding composes a group, which composes its members. At one level "no part used
#   twice" is a key. At two it becomes "no layer at the bottom reachable by two paths", and the
#   second path runs through a document the first does not contain.
#
# No depth limit and no guess
#   The `CYCLE` clause stops the recursion exactly when a layer repeats within its own path, which
#   is what would otherwise keep a reader walking for ever, and `path` records the route that came
#   round as data. A depth limit is a guess about how deep a legitimate composition goes; this is
#   the actual question.
#
# A diamond is not a loop in the sense this walk stops on
#   One layer reached down two different branches is not a repeat within a path, so it still comes
#   back twice, which is what the rule on overlapping parts needs. A walk with a depth limit cuts
#   it short, and a walk without the clause never returns on a document that closes a loop. It is a
#   loop in another sense, ignoring direction, which `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` counts, and there it
#   is the double count itself. Both readings are about the same diamond and neither is wrong: this
#   walk must carry it, and `checks/jagged_layer` must accuse it. Say which loop is meant wherever
#   the word is used.
#
# Four meanings of the word, and four kinds of thing
#   The word cycle carries four meanings in these queries, and the first three are different kinds
#   of thing, not readings of one:
#     1. A loop ignoring direction. `rank/cycle_space.sqlc` counts these; `checks/jagged_layer`
#        holds the layer graph's at zero, and `checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close` makes the
#        unit graph's come back to where it started.
#     2. A loop following direction: layers that each reach the other. That is
#        `checks/layers_move_together`, and it is also SQL's own word: the
#        `CYCLE ... SET is_cycle` clause below stops on this meaning and not on the first.
#     3. The window, the part of its period a supply is live. It is a quantity rather than a shape,
#        and the only one of the four that can say the question does not apply: a stock has no
#        period to be live in, so it takes `notApplicable` rather than an omission.
#     4. The loop you can walk round, which is what the other three come to. The first says a loop
#        exists, the second says it can be walked the way the arrows point, and the third gives the
#        period it comes back after.
#
# Whether a walk round comes back depends on what is compared
#   Walk the unit graph's one loop and you arrive back at the unit you started from, multiplied. As
#   a unit it came back; as a size it did not. So "does it come back" has no answer until it is
#   said which is meant, the same care every classification here takes.
#   `checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close` decides it for the unit graph without adjusting a
#   filed number: it asks whether the failure to come back can be told apart from none, given the
#   width each factor was filed with.
#
# The product of the factors turns a recursion into a join
#   It carries the product of the factors along each path. A composed layer's remainder is defined
#   level by level through the parts, and the natural form needs a sum in the recursive step, which
#   SQL forbids. A running product is one value per row and is allowed, so the walk can carry the
#   product on the way down, and one `GROUP BY` at the end replaces the recursion entirely.
#
# `factor_absent` is carried, never filled in
#   A part whose conversion nobody sized, or filed as an identity's output that nothing here
#   computes, makes every path through it unknown: the product is unknown rather than one, and a
#   caller that reads the number without the flag has asserted the units already agree.
#
# Who reads the product
#   `composition/leaves.sqlc`, `composition/reachable.sqlc`, `rank/co_moving_layers.sqlc`,
#   `rank/evaluation_order.sqlc`, `algebra/leaves.sqlc` and `matrices.sql`'s tour take this walk's
#   keys, its depth, its path and its loop flag. `rank/composition_closure.sqlc` also reads the
#   product and its flag, to ask whether every layer at the bottom is reached through a positive
#   product. The remainder carries the same product on its own walk,
#   `composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc`, and from there `composition/settled_remainders.sqlc` and
#   `composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc` read it.
#
# Do not merge it with the remainder frontier
#   `composition/remainder_frontier.sqlc` is this walk with a stop condition, and a reader who finds
#   the two nearly identical should read that file's header rather than merge them: a remainder
#   bottoms out earlier than a sum does, and stopping inside a recursion is not a filter over this
#   relation's result.
#
# The cycle clause's own path is not returned
#   `CYCLE ... USING route` keeps the path it walked as a column of a pseudo-type. The walk returns
#   `is_cycle` and every column of its own, and not that one.
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part followed through every level by way of pm.filing_identity.
WITH RECURSIVE
resolved AS (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
),
walk(root_filing, root_layer, filing, layer, depth, path,
     factor_low, factor_mode, factor_high, factor_absent) AS (
        SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer, 1,
               ARRAY[p.composition  || '/' || p.composed_layer,
                     p.part_filing  || '/' || p.part_layer],
               coalesce(p.factor_low, 1), coalesce(p.factor_mode, 1), coalesce(p.factor_high, 1),
               p.factor_state IN ('absent', 'derivation')
        FROM resolved p
    UNION ALL
        SELECT w.root_filing, w.root_layer, p.part_filing, p.part_layer, w.depth + 1,
               w.path || (p.part_filing || '/' || p.part_layer),
               w.factor_low  * coalesce(p.factor_low,  1),
               w.factor_mode * coalesce(p.factor_mode, 1),
               w.factor_high * coalesce(p.factor_high, 1),
               w.factor_absent OR p.factor_state IN ('absent', 'derivation')
        FROM walk w
        JOIN resolved p ON p.composition = w.filing AND p.composed_layer = w.layer
) CYCLE filing, layer SET is_cycle USING route
SELECT root_filing, root_layer, filing, layer, depth, path,
       factor_low, factor_mode, factor_high, factor_absent, is_cycle
FROM walk
